  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {{(\\sqrt{u})}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({(\\sqrt{1})}^{3}-{(\\sqrt{0})}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
